## 4. Security‑Best‑Practice Enhancements

| Concern | Why it matters | Quick fix |
|---------|----------------|-----------|
| **Helmet** – sets secure HTTP headers | Mitigates clickjacking, MIME sniffing, CSP, etc. | `npm i helmet` then `app.use(require('helmet')());` |
| **Rate limiting** – prevents brute‑force / DoS | Throttles abusive clients | `npm i express-rate-limit` and configure a limiter for all routes. |
| **CORS** – restricts which origins can call your API | Avoids unwanted cross‑origin calls | `app.use(require('cors')({ origin: process.env.ALLOWED_ORIGIN || 'https://myapp.example.com' }));` |
| **Body parsing limits** – avoid massive payload attacks | Set size caps on JSON/url‑encoded bodies | `app.use(express.json({ limit: '100kb' }));` |
| **Hidden error details** – don’t expose stack traces in production | Prevents information leakage | In the error middleware, send a generic message when `process.env.NODE_ENV !== 'development'`. |
| **Graceful handling of unknown routes** – avoid 404 leaks | Consistent JSON error response for any unmapped path | ```js\napp.use((req, res) => res.status(404).json({ error: 'Not Found' }));\n``` |
| **Environment configuration** – keep secrets out of code | Use `.env` + `dotenv` and never commit secrets | `require('dotenv').config();` and add `.env*` to `.gitignore`. |
| **Dependency audit** – detect vulnerable packages | Run `npm audit` regularly or use `npm audit fix`. | Automate in CI. |
| **HTTPS enforcement** – ensure TLS in production | Prevents man‑in‑the‑middle attacks | If behind a proxy, set `app.enable('trust proxy')` and redirect HTTP → HTTPS in production (`if (req.secure) next(); else res.redirect('https://' + req.headers.host + req.url);`). |
| **Logging** – structured logs for monitoring | Helps detect abnormal activity | Use a logger like **pino** or **winston** instead of `console.log`. |

### Sample “secure baseline” setup

```js
require('dotenv').config();          // Load .env
const express = require('express');
const helmet = require('helmet');
const cors = require('cors');
const rateLimit = require('express-rate-limit');
const pino = require('pino-http')(); // Structured request logger

const app = express();

// ----- Middleware stack -----
app.use(pino);                        // request logging
app.use(helmet());                    // secure headers
app.use(cors({                         // lock down origins
  origin: process.env.ALLOWED_ORIGIN || 'https://myapp.example.com',
  methods: ['GET', 'POST', 'PUT', 'DELETE'],
}));
app.use(express.json({ limit: '100kb' })); // body size limit
app.use(express.urlencoded({ extended: false }));

// Rate limiter (example: 100 req / 15 min per IP)
const limiter = rateLimit({
  windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000,
  max: 100,
  standardHeaders: true,
  legacyHeaders: false,
});
app.use(limiter);

// ------ Routes (keep them after the middleware) ------
app.get('/health', (req, res) => {
  res.json({ status: 'ok', timestamp: new Date().toISOString() });
});

app.get('/users', (req, res) => {
  res.json([
    { id: 1, name: 'Alice', email: 'alice@example.com' },
    { id: 2, name: 'Bob', email: 'bob@example.com' },
  ]);
});

// 404 handler
app.use((req, res) => {
  res.status(404).json({ error: 'Not Found' });
});

// Central error handler (same as in §2.1)
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  console.error(err);
  const status = err.status || 500;
  const message =
    process.env.NODE_ENV === 'development' ? err.message : 'Internal Server Error';
  res.status(status).json({ error: message });
});

const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;
app.listen(PORT, () => {
  console.log(`Server running on port ${PORT}`);
});

module.exports = app;
```

