## 2️⃣ “What could go wrong?” – Security & Stability Risks

| Area | Risk | Why it matters |
|------|------|----------------|
| **Missing error‑handling middleware** | Uncaught exceptions become 500 responses that may include stack traces. | Reveals internal implementation details to an attacker and can crash the process. |
| **No request‑size limits / rate‑limiting** | Unlimited requests can cause DoS or brute‑force attempts. | Simple “/users” endpoint could be hammered; later POST endpoints could be abused. |
| **No HTTP‑header hardening** | Missing `X‑Content‑Type‑Options`, `Strict‑Transport‑Security`, etc. | Facilitates MIME‑sniffing, click‑jacking, and other classic web attacks. |
| **No input validation / sanitisation** | Future POST/PUT routes could accept malicious payloads (SQLi, XSS, prototype pollution). | Bad data can reach downstream services or cause server‑side injection. |
| **Hard‑coded port / plain console logging** | Makes it harder to run behind a process manager or to track audit events. | Production environments need structured logs and configurable ports. |
| **No HTTPS enforcement** | App could be accessed over clear‑text HTTP. | Credentials, tokens, or personal data could be sniffed. |
| **No CORS policy** | By default Express allows any origin (depends on environment). | May expose data to malicious front‑ends. |
| **Missing Helmet** | Express doesn’t set secure defaults. | You have to manually set dozens of headers. |
| **No async‑error handling** | `await`‑ed code in future routes could reject without being caught. | Leads to silent request failures. |

